From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Wu Yuwei <yuweiwu@pm.me>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 18:03:18 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Index styles with u32

A layout's styles were indexed with `u16`, so pushing the 65,536th
style panicked with `too many styles` in `TreeBuilder`, `RangedBuilder`
and the style run builder. A browser gets there with an ordinary page:
an inline element per word or two over a long article, each element a
span and each span a style, or more than one when the embedder tells
runs apart with styles of its own. Blink has no such limit: an inline
item points at its layout object, and the style comes from there.

Every style index is a `u32` now: the builders' style tables,
`char_style_indices`, the characters and clusters the shaper makes, and
the style metrics' and line breaker's references to styles. The three
builders still panic with `too many styles` past `u32::MAX`, through a
checked conversion.

`Character` and `ShapedCluster`, which a layout keeps, keep their sizes:
the index takes padding that was there already. `Char`, which exists only
while a layout is built, grows from 8 to 12 bytes, and
`char_style_indices` from 2 to 4 bytes a character. Across
`parley_bench` the median change is +1%; the glyph-run benches with a
new style every 16 characters are about 10% slower.

Tests: `builders_take_more_styles_than_a_u16_indexes` lays out 70,000
styles with each of the three builders and checks that the last glyph
run has the last style and is shaped with its font: the last style has
a family the others don't, which an index that wraps would miss.
